0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","2.2",{"topic":{"id":"2.2","title":"Stages of Cyber Crime Investigation Process","minutes":14,"summary":"Move from complaint to report through iterative, documented stages without treating a process diagram as a substitute for judgment.","sections":[{"title":"Intake, triage, and preservation","body":["Intake captures the complaint, identities, incident time, affected systems, financial loss, and available records. Triage prioritizes immediate danger, ongoing theft, evidence volatility, and legal deadlines. Verify contact details independently rather than calling a number supplied by a suspected scammer. Assign a case identifier and record the initial state without promising a result.","Preservation can begin with the complainant’s original messages, transaction records, and relevant device state. Request provider preservation through the proper channel when necessary. A preservation request does not itself disclose content. Record the exact scope and receipt time. Avoid editing original files or asking a victim to forward material repeatedly until provenance becomes unclear."]},{"title":"Collection, examination, and analysis","body":["Collection acquires identified material using a suitable, authorized method. Examination extracts and organizes relevant artifacts from a verified working copy. Analysis interprets relationships, sequences, and competing explanations. These stages are related but distinct: finding a filename is an observation; concluding that a particular person intentionally created it requires additional evidence.","NIST SP 800-86 describes collection, examination, analysis, and reporting. Other models use identification, preservation, acquisition, analysis, and presentation. The terminology matters less than preserving integrity, documenting decisions, and making the process reproducible. Revisit collection when analysis reveals a new relevant source, but update the authority and scope where needed."]},{"title":"Reporting, review, and feedback","body":["Reporting connects each conclusion to an exhibit, method, and limitation. A second examiner should be able to understand tool versions, settings, time normalization, and material transformations. Prosecutors and investigators may need clarification or further lawful collection. Evidence should remain available for independent examination under appropriate safeguards.","Close the case operationally without destroying evidence merely because a system is restored. Retention, return, and disposal require legal and organizational authority. Capture lessons: which logs were missing, which request was late, and which control would have reduced harm? An investigation should improve future prevention as well as address the present allegation."]}],"practice":{"title":"Sequence an invoice-fraud case","scenario":"You have a complaint, a mailbox export, a bank reference, and a suspect laptop, but no acquisition yet.","steps":["Record intake and preserve the available originals.","Define authority and prioritize volatile or short-lived records.","Acquire and verify the laptop through a suitable method.","Analyze the message and payment timeline, then draft a source-linked report."],"debrief":"The stages can overlap. The important feature is that every transition has a documented purpose and does not silently alter the evidence."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bnss","bsa"],"sources":["nist-guide","cert","bnss"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":2,"objectives":["Explain intake, triage, and preservation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain collection, examination, and analysis and apply it to an investigation.","Explain reporting, review, and feedback and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-2.2","prompt":"How does examination differ from analysis?","options":["Examination always proves intent; analysis does not.","They are identical operations.","Examination organizes artifacts; analysis evaluates their meaning and competing explanations.","Analysis occurs before any evidence is collected."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Extracting an artifact and explaining its investigative significance are separate intellectual steps.","unit":2}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
